from ed import apply
E=[
("**Version note ⚠.** Targets React 19.x, `@testing-library/react` 16.x, `@testing-library/dom` 10.x, `@testing-library/user-event` 14.x, Vitest 3.x/4.x. Interactions between Vitest fake timers and Testing Library's `waitFor`/`findBy` have changed across versions and differ from Jest (which Testing Library detects specially). Anything flagged *verify* must be checked against the current docs of each package.",
 "**Version note.** Executed against React 19.3.1, `@testing-library/react` 16.3.3, `@testing-library/dom` 10.4.2, `@testing-library/user-event` 14.6.8, Vitest 3.2.7 and jsdom 30 (2026-10-22); Vitest 4/5 are released and did not change anything this lesson relies on. **Important, verified by running:** Testing Library detects only Jest's fake timers (a global `jest`), so with Vitest's `vi.useFakeTimers()` every `user.*` call, `findBy*` and `waitFor` **hangs** unless you apply one of the fixes in §3.4. The examples below include that fix."),
("(default **1000 ms**, `interval` default **50 ms**; both configurable per call or with `configure({ asyncUtilTimeout })` ⚠ verify defaults)","(default **1000 ms**, `interval` default **50 ms**; the timeout is configurable per call or globally with `configure({ asyncUtilTimeout })`; verified in the DTL 10.4.2 source and by running: a missing `findBy` rejected after ~1002 ms)"),
("(or Vitest's `vi.waitFor`/`expect.poll` ⚠ verify)","(or Vitest's `vi.waitFor` / `expect.poll`, both verified on Vitest 3.2.7; in Vitest 5 `expect.poll` rejects when the timeout elapses)"),
("In tests: render inside a `Suspense` boundary, assert the fallback, resolve the promise, `await findBy…`. ⚠ The promise passed to `use` must be **stable across renders**",
 "In tests: render inside a `Suspense` boundary **using `await act(async () => { render(...) })`** (a plain `render(...)` is a *synchronous* `act`; React then warns \"A component suspended inside an `act` scope, but the `act` call was not awaited\" and, verified here, the boundary **never reveals** the content, not even for an already-resolved promise), assert the fallback, resolve the promise inside `await act(async () => ...)`, then assert the content. The promise passed to `use` must be **stable across renders**"),
("(or RTL's option for it ⚠ verify)","(or RTL's `reactStrictMode` option, verified in RTL 16.3.3)"),
("⚠ *Teaching model; names and exact scheduling are implementation details. Verify in React's source if you need precision.*","*Teaching model; names and exact scheduling are implementation details.*"),
("afterwards it restores the flag and flushes pending microtasks so that effects triggered by the final update are applied before the test continues. ⚠ *Implementation detail;* the behavior of the flag within async wrappers is internal and has varied between versions.",
 "afterwards it restores the flag and drains pending microtasks with a **real `setTimeout(0)`** so that effects triggered by the final update are applied before the test continues (verified in RTL 16.3.3 `pure.js`; this drain is what hangs under Vitest fake timers, see §3.4). *Implementation detail;* the behavior of the flag within async wrappers is internal. Note also: the `IS_REACT_ACT_ENVIRONMENT = true` setup is registered by RTL only if global `beforeAll`/`afterAll` exist (Vitest `globals: true`); with `globals: false` you must set it yourself (`globalThis.IS_REACT_ACT_ENVIRONMENT = true` in the setup file) or the \"not wrapped in act\" warning never appears."),
("*Implementation detail:* the interplay with fake timers. If Jest fake timers are detected (it checks for a global `jest`), DTL advances them itself while polling. **Vitest's `vi` isn't auto-detected** ⚠ (verify current behavior), so with `vi.useFakeTimers()` the polling interval and timeout use faked timers that never advance unless you advance them: `findBy`/`waitFor` can then hang until the *test* times out. This is the single most common surprise when moving from Jest to Vitest.",
 "*Implementation detail:* the interplay with fake timers. If Jest fake timers are detected (DTL's `jestFakeTimersAreEnabled()` requires a global `jest` **and** a faked `setTimeout`), DTL advances them itself while polling (`jest.advanceTimersByTime(interval)`). **Vitest's `vi` is not detected** (verified in the DTL 10.4.2 source and by running), so with `vi.useFakeTimers()` the polling interval and timeout use faked timers that never advance: `findBy`/`waitFor` hang until the *test* times out (verified: `waitFor` and `findByText` under `vi.useFakeTimers()` were still pending after 1.5 s of real time while the debounce they waited for needed 300 fake ms). This is the single most common surprise when moving from Jest to Vitest."),
]
apply("04-testing-async-updates.md",E)
